**Design notes**

| Concern | How it is handled |
|---|---|
| **Encryption at rest** | Every value is sealed with AES-256-GCM using a key derived from the master password via PBKDF2 (100 k iterations, random 32-byte salt persisted in `_meta/salt`). Only the base64 length of `nonce ‖ ciphertext ‖ tag` is visible, leaking at most the plaintext length + 28 bytes. |
| **Owner / name isolation** | Each secret lives under its own storage key `secret/<SHA-256(⌊len⌋owner ‖ owner ‖ ⌊len⌋name)>`. The same hash is used as GCM *additional authenticated data*, so copying one secret's blob onto another owner/name key causes a tag mismatch and `get` throws. |
| **Tamper detection** | GCM's 128-bit authentication tag detects any bit-flip in the ciphertext, nonce, or tag. If the attacker swaps in another secret's blob, the AAD no longer matches and decryption fails. All failure paths in `get` re-throw a plain `Error`. |
| **Wrong master password** | PBKDF2 derives a different AES key, so every GCM decrypt fails and `get` throws—satisfying the "reject or throw" requirement without needing to validate at construction time. |
| **Arbitrary characters** | Owner/name are UTF-8 encoded with 4-byte length prefixes before hashing, eliminating separator collisions. The per-owner index (`index/<SHA-256(owner)>`) stores names as a JSON array for `list`. |
| **No imports** | Only the global `crypto.subtle`, `crypto.getRandomValues`, `TextEncoder/Decoder`, `btoa/atob`, `JSON`, `DataView`, and typed arrays are used. |

